 "assumption": "Referral sensitivity = 0.5: how strongly referrals fall as the waiting time rises above its normal value.",
 "why": "In equilibrium Referrals = Treatments = Capacity C, and Waiting time W = Waiting list / C. The referral equation then gives 4000 (W/8)^(-s) = C, so W = 8 (4000/C)^(1/s) and the list L = C W = 8 C (4000/C)^(1/s). Extra capacity shortens the list only if s < 1. At s = 1 capacity cancels and the list settles at 8 x 4000 = 32,000 whatever the capacity; for s > 1 more capacity makes the list longer. The small effect in the result comes from s = 0.5 sitting between these cases, and nothing in the model pins s down.",
 "prediction": "With s = 0 the extra capacity removes most of the list; at s = 1 the list is the same with and without it; at s = 2 it is longer with it.",
 "test": {
  "parameter": "Referral sensitivity",
  "values": [
   0,
   0.25,
   0.5,
   1,
   2
  ]
 }
}
